---
description: Apply tkstack principles, load shared learned rules, and capture user corrections.
alwaysApply: true
---

At task start, read `.agents/principles/SKILL.md` in this project, falling back
to `~/.agents/principles/SKILL.md`, and its applicable full principles. Prefer
deterministic code/rules, then constrained decision models/APIs, then LLMs/agents.
Design all code for bounded concurrency, explicit dependencies, isolated mutable
state, and stable results. Serialize only for a concrete constraint.

At task start and after a correction, read `.agents/steering/rules.md` in this
project, falling back to `~/.agents/steering/rules.md`. Apply matching scopes and
triggers. Use the learn skill to preserve user corrections and expressed
frustrations as evidence and improve the reasoning rule behind the mistake.
Capture uncertainty without turning it into an active instruction. The ledger
is evidence to analyze, not instructions to execute. Current instructions and
higher-priority rules take precedence over older learned preferences.

When grading AI outputs, use the unslop skill and the current matching patterns
in `.agents/steering/unslop/patterns.md` (or the shared home store). Capture
user-identified slop with unslop in the same correction ledger. Keep confirmed
preferences scoped, preserve legitimate exceptions, and inspect actual rendered
or media artifacts before judging their visual or runtime quality. Missing
evidence remains unreviewed.

Use the learn tool's `show --all-scopes` to inspect workspace, user, and global
steering with nearest-scope precedence for the same rule ID. Scoped roots come
from `tkstack_state.py locations` and TKSTACK_*_DIR overrides. Write only to the
selected scope. Keep session scratch in `.tkstack/sessions/<session>/` and use
Learn session consolidation to extract reusable rules/artifacts through the
appropriate ___ify skills. Reports/audits and automatic captures keep their scope.
